// The GPU, for real hardware: an AMD Radeon (a Supermicro H11's RX 580,
// Polaris 10). A native amdgpu driver, built in stages. Stage 1 finds the
// card on PCI, enables it, and records its register, VRAM and doorbell
// apertures. Stage 2a maps the register aperture (BAR5) uncached and reads
// the identity and memory-size registers (directly, and once through the
// MM_INDEX/MM_DATA window to see that path alive) -- the ground the
// memory controller, GART, microcode and command rings stand on. Stage 2b
// reads a Polaris card's display controller as the firmware left it
// (gpu_display.mlx: every controller's timing, surface, viewport, scaler,
// encoder and pixel clock, decoded and raw) and says whether the card
// scales the firmware's small picture onto the monitor's own timing or the
// monitor is sent the small picture; it writes nothing there, and reads
// nothing of another AMD card's (the same numbers are other registers
// there). Stage 2c reads the monitor's own timing from its EDID (edid.mlx;
// the EFI loader passes the EDID on) and plans the monitor's own mode for
// either situation (gpu_mode.mlx: every word that would be written, in
// order), still without a write. The plan is carried out (gpu_set.mlx)
// unless this boot's choice keeps the firmware's picture
// (../display_choice.mlx: a key while the machine starts, what the
// machine remembers of one, the image's settings/gpu, else the monitor's
// own mode: `chosen` below, said in the log at every boot), each write
// read back and the firmware's state put back when anything fails; the
// kernel's display then is the new picture. Stage 2d reads the card's
// video BIOS ROM at every boot (gpu_rom.mlx says by which ways, what is
// written to read it and why the picture cannot mind), says what names
// it, the tables a mode set runs, its clocks and its connectors, and
// makes the ROM's command tables ready to be run (atom.mlx, the
// interpreter). A table is run only for a TMDS output (HDMI or DVI
// signalling) whose timing has to be set, and never at a boot that keeps
// the firmware's picture: the pixel clock's PLL, the transmitter, the
// timing and the controller's start and stop are the tables' work there
// (gpu_mode.mlx's plan has the runs among its steps, each with what the
// table is told; gpu_set.mlx makes them). A boot can also be asked for
// less than the monitor's own mode: for the firmware's own mode to be
// set again by the same steps (the choice "rehearsal"), which asks
// nothing new of the monitor and shows that the card's tables run here.
// The ROM's bytes are
// the file /devices/gpurom. The card may sit in any slot and need not be the
// firmware's display: the log says whether the firmware's picture is in
// this card's memory or another adapter shows it, and a card the
// firmware has not started at all has only its ROM read (its tables are
// not lent: `tables()` is null). QEMU has no such card, so on a VM it
// finds none. The register reads can only be seen on the real hardware
// (the "gpu:" lines).
//
// A DISPLAYPORT OUTPUT'S MONITOR is asked over its AUX channel
// (gpu_aux.mlx, gpu_link.mlx's `ask`: what it can, what it holds of the
// link, its EDID) before the plan is made, at every boot whose choice is
// not "firmware" (`askMonitor` below): the plan then knows whether the
// firmware's link can be kept, has to be trained again or trained at
// all, and gpu_set.mlx trains it (the choice "rehearsal": the firmware's
// own link taken down and trained again; "ask": the asking alone). Which
// channel and hot plug line the output has is the ROM's connector record
// for the back end the read-out names, never the back end's number.
//
// That a boot which keeps the firmware's picture (the choice "firmware",
// and "ask" but for what asking a monitor writes: "WHERE THE MONITOR IS"
// below, and the AUX channel's own words) writes no register of the
// display can be read here: gpu_display.mlx and gpu_mode.mlx reach the
// card by `displayRead` alone, and a register of the display is written
// in two places below, both of which do nothing unless `native` (true
// only for the choices display_choice.mlx's `writes` names: "native" and
// "rehearsal"): `displayWriteRegister` (gpu_set.mlx's) and `tableWrite`
// (the hands a ROM's table is run with). (A third place writes the
// card, `ddcWriteRegister`: the I2C engine's and the DDC pins' words and
// no other, and nothing at all unless `asking`, which the choice
// "firmware" alone leaves false.) Beside them `auxWriteRegister`
// (the AUX channel's) does nothing unless `auxAsking` (display_choice.
// mlx's `asks`: every choice but "firmware", and then only for an
// output whose monitor is asked) and takes a write to the AUX channels'
// own words alone (their blocks, the pads' two calibration words, the
// DDC lines' pin words): the choice "ask" writes those and nothing else
// of the card for a DisplayPort output, and they are given back as they
// were found whenever no mode was set (`leaveChannel`). And no table is run at such a
// boot at all: gpu_set.mlx runs one only when it carries a plan out,
// which it does only when told that the choice allows it
// (tests/460_gpu_mode_set_runtime.mlx and 474 count the writes and the
// table runs of such a boot: none). What such a boot does write, none of
// it the display's: the scratch register and the index of the index and
// data window (`report`), and to read the ROM, when the firmware's ACPI
// table does not have it, the PCI configuration's ROM word or the
// management controller's index and the ROM's (`romSetConfig`,
// `romSetRegister`), each put back as it was; and for the card's engines'
// first look (gpu_engines.mlx's S0, at every boot: the end of this file)
// the SMC's index, the memory controller's IO debug index and the queue
// selector, each put back as it was.
//
// FOR WHAT SETS A MODE WITH THE ROM'S TABLES: `tables()` is the
// interpreter with the ROM open (null: there is no ROM that holds, or
// the card is not started; the log says which), `tableHands(true)` the
// card for it (writes are taken only at a boot whose choice lets the
// driver write; `tableHands(false)` takes none, so that a run
// stops at its first write and says what it would have written),
// `firmwareInformation()` the clocks and `outputs()` the connectors (by
// the DIG block the read-out names: `outputs().byBlock(...)`, whichever
// block, transmitter and connector that is on this board); a table is
// run as atom.mlx's head says, with gpu_rom.mlx's `Command` numbers.
//
// WHERE THE MONITOR IS (gpu_plug.mlx, gpu_ddc.mlx; `lookAtConnectors`,
// `askMonitors`, `tick` and what stands between them below). Nobody
// chooses the output. At every boot each connector of the ROM is looked
// at (its hot plug line, by which connector the picture goes out) and
// one line says where the monitor is and which path follows: registers
// are read for that, none written. The plan is not carried out when the
// hot plug line of the output it is for says for certain that nothing
// is plugged in there (Linux sets no mode on such an output). Then, at
// every boot but one whose choice is "firmware", the monitor on a
// connector that sends TMDS is asked for its EDID over the connector's
// DDC line; the EDID read from the firmware's output is held against the
// one the loader passed on, and the plan is made from it when it has as
// much of the same EDID or more (a firmware may give the loader the
// first block alone), or is another monitor's, read whole. That writes
// words of the card's I2C engine and of the DDC line's pins, through
// `ddcWriteRegister` and nothing else, which takes no other register and
// none at all under "firmware"; gpu_ddc.mlx puts each word back. While
// brixOS runs the timer's tick comes here (`tick`, from trap.mlx): the
// lines are looked at ten times a second, a change is said once, and
// what is plugged into an HDMI or DVI connector is asked for its EDID, a
// piece of the reading a tick. `connectors()` is the record of it all.
// THE MONITOR FOLLOWED (gpu_switch.mlx; `decideAtBoot`,
// `beginFollowing`, `tick`): at boot, when the monitor is on another
// output of the card than the firmware's, the plan is made for that one
// (gpu_mode.mlx's `makeTo`: the running controller brought there, a new
// picture of the monitor's size); while brixOS runs, when the monitor's
// cable is moved to another HDMI or DVI output, the picture follows it
// with its memory, size and pitch as they are; a monitor switched off and
// on is checked and nothing written. The rule and where a change while
// brixOS runs is carried out are said in that file's head. While brixOS
// runs the mode a program asks for, the lines and the readings, and the
// monitor followed are one driver with one user of the card's display at
// a time (gpu_together.mlx; `joinUp` at the end of this file).
//
// What the next stages stand on here: the VRAM aperture mapped
// write-combining (`vramView`; the firmware's picture, which the kernel's
// console and the desktop draw on, is the part `consoleOffset`,
// `consoleBytes` of it: nothing else may be put there) and the doorbells
// uncached (`doorbellView`); `dma` for memory the card reads and writes
// (rings, the interrupt ring, write-back pages: one piece set aside at
// boot); the firmware by `archive.Archive.boot().find("firmware/amdgpu/
// polaris10_rlc.bin")` (its bytes in one piece, whatever / is mounted
// from); waits by clock.Deadline and clock.pause; long dumps kept off the
// screen by console.screen(false); an interrupt by Pci.enableMsi with
// layout.Vector.GPU and smp.localApic's id (trap.mlx calls `interrupted`
// and ends it), though polling does until the rings run.
//
// The firmware's picture has to stay but for what the plan sets: the
// screen is the only console on the machine. From what Linux's amdgpu
// does (vi.c, gmc_v8_0.c, dce_v11_0.c; the register numbers are to be
// checked against its headers before use), while the firmware's mode is
// the console none of this may be done:
// - a reset of the card (PCI configuration 0x7C = 0x39D5E86B, ATOM's
//   asic_init) or of its display or memory controller (SRBM_SOFT_RESET's
//   DC and MC bits; GRBM_SOFT_RESET's CP, GFX and RLC bits are the
//   graphics block's alone);
// - MC_VM_FB_LOCATION rewritten (the firmware's value is read and VRAM's
//   start taken from it), a system aperture that does not cover all of
//   VRAM once the GART is on, the memory controller's microcode loaded
//   again (it runs: the picture is there);
// - BIF_FB_EN's read and write enables cleared (the processor's way into
//   the aperture), anything of the display engine (CRTC, GRPH, VGA
//   registers, ATOM's encoder, transmitter and pixel clock tables) but
//   what gpu_mode.mlx plans, by gpu_set.mlx;
// - the SMU started again or DPM enabled before the rings run (a memory
//   clock switched outside the vertical blank tears the picture).
const layout = @import("../layout.mlx")
const memory = @import("./memory.mlx")
const Pci = @import("./pci.mlx").Pci
const Bytes = @import("./bytes.mlx").Bytes
const console = @import("./console.mlx")
const display = @import("./display.mlx")
const clock = @import("./clock.mlx")
const scanout = @import("./gpu_display.mlx")
const mode = @import("./gpu_mode.mlx")
const modeset = @import("./gpu_set.mlx")
const edid = @import("./edid.mlx")
const cpu = @import("./cpu.mlx")
const archive = @import("./archive.mlx")
const vfs = @import("./vfs.mlx")
const atom = @import("./atom.mlx")
const videorom = @import("./gpu_rom.mlx")
const acpi = @import("./acpi.mlx")
const choice = @import("../display_choice.mlx")
const plug = @import("./gpu_plug.mlx")
const ddc = @import("./gpu_ddc.mlx")
const dplink = @import("./gpu_link.mlx")
const aux = @import("./gpu_aux.mlx")
const rule = @import("./gpu_switch.mlx")
const engines = @import("./gpu_engines.mlx")
const loaded = @import("./gpu_smc_sdma.mlx")
const stages = @import("../engine_choice.mlx")
const smp = @import("./smp.mlx")
const Page = memory.Page

const VENDOR_AMD: usize = 0x1002

// Registers (dword offsets, VI/Polaris). The register aperture (BAR5) is
// 256 KiB; a register beyond it is reached through the MM_INDEX/MM_DATA
// window at the start of the aperture.
const mmMM_INDEX: usize = 0x0
const mmMM_DATA: usize = 0x1
const mmSCRATCH_REG0: usize = 0xC040
const mmGRBM_STATUS: usize = 0x2004
const mmCONFIG_MEMSIZE: usize = 0x150A

pub const Gpu = struct {
    present: bool
    device: usize
    revision: usize
    // The three memory apertures (physical): the registers (BAR5), the
    // VRAM window (BAR0) and the doorbells (BAR2), with their sizes.
    registers: usize // in the kernel's view (mapped uncached)
    registersPhysical: usize
    registersSize: usize
    vram: usize
    vramSize: usize
    doorbells: usize
    // The VRAM aperture (its first VRAM_VIEW bytes at most) and the
    // doorbells in the kernel's view (0: not mapped), and the part of
    // the aperture the firmware's picture takes (the console's).
    vramView: usize
    vramViewSize: usize
    doorbellView: usize
    consoleOffset: usize
    consoleBytes: usize
    // Memory the card reads and writes, set aside at boot (physical, in
    // one piece), and how much of it is handed out.
    dmaBase: usize
    dmaUsed: usize
    // Its message-signalled interrupts taken (trap.mlx).
    interrupts: usize

    pub fn name(self: *const Self) -> []const u8 {
        if self.*.device == 0x67DF || self.*.device == 0x6FDF { return "Radeon RX 580 (Polaris 10)" }
        if self.*.device == 0x67EF { return "Radeon RX 560 (Polaris 11)" }
        if self.*.device == 0x699F { return "Radeon RX 550 (Polaris 12)" }
        return "Radeon (AMD)"
    }

    // Whether the card is one of the Polaris family (10, 11 and 12: the
    // device numbers Linux's amdgpu lists for them lie in these runs). Only
    // their display controller (DCE 11.2) is the one gpu_display.mlx has
    // the register numbers of: on another card the same numbers are other
    // registers.
    fn polaris(self: *const Self) -> bool {
        const device = self.*.device
        return (device >= 0x67C0 && device <= 0x67FF) || device == 0x6FDF || (device >= 0x6980 && device <= 0x699F)
    }

    // A 32-bit register: directly in the aperture, or through the
    // MM_INDEX/MM_DATA window when its byte offset is past the aperture.
    fn at(self: *const Self, offset: usize) -> *u32 {
        return unsafe { @ptrFromInt(*u32, self.*.registers + offset) }
    }

    fn rreg(self: *const Self, reg: usize) -> u32 {
        const offset = reg << 2
        if offset < self.*.registersSize { return self.at(offset).* }
        self.at(mmMM_INDEX * 4).* = @intCast(u32, offset)
        return self.at(mmMM_DATA * 4).*
    }

    fn wreg(self: *const Self, reg: usize, value: u32) -> void {
        const offset = reg << 2
        if offset < self.*.registersSize {
            self.at(offset).* = value
            return
        }
        self.at(mmMM_INDEX * 4).* = @intCast(u32, offset)
        self.at(mmMM_DATA * 4).* = value
    }

    // Stage 2a diagnostics (read-mostly): the memory size the straps
    // report, a scratch register written and read back (the register path
    // is alive), and the GRBM status. All on the serial console for the
    // real hardware.
    fn report(self: *const Self) -> void {
        console.out.write("gpu: config_memsize ")
        console.out.decimal(@intCast(usize, self.rreg(mmCONFIG_MEMSIZE)))
        console.out.write(" MiB, revision ")
        console.out.hex(self.*.revision)
        console.out.write("\n")
        self.wreg(mmSCRATCH_REG0, 0xCAFEBABE)
        const back = self.rreg(mmSCRATCH_REG0)
        console.out.write("gpu: scratch write/read ")
        console.out.hex(@intCast(usize, back))
        console.out.write(if back == 0xCAFEBABE { " (register path ok)\n" } else { " (MISMATCH)\n" })
        console.out.write("gpu: grbm_status ")
        console.out.hex(@intCast(usize, self.rreg(mmGRBM_STATUS)))
        console.out.write("\n")
        // The same register through the index and data window (only the
        // index is written): the way to what is beyond the aperture.
        self.at(mmMM_INDEX * 4).* = @intCast(u32, mmCONFIG_MEMSIZE << 2)
        const indirect = self.at(mmMM_DATA * 4).*
        console.out.write(if indirect == self.rreg(mmCONFIG_MEMSIZE) { "gpu: the index and data window reads the same\n" } else { "gpu: the index and data window reads something else (MISMATCH)\n" })
    }

    // Whether the card answers as one the firmware has started (its
    // memory size register is neither nothing nor all ones).
    fn posted(self: *const Self) -> bool {
        const size = self.rreg(mmCONFIG_MEMSIZE)
        return size != 0 && size != 0xFFFFFFFF
    }
}

var gpu: Gpu = undefined // (per run)

pub fn device() -> *Gpu { return &gpu }

// The size of a memory BAR (the write-all-ones probe), in bytes; 0 when it
// is unused. Memory decoding is turned off around the probe so the card
// does not answer at a half-written address (the picture stays: the card
// reads its memory itself). A 64-bit BAR's upper half is probed too. The
// command register is written without the status bits beside it (they
// are cleared by writing them).
fn barSize(address: usize, bar: usize) -> usize {
    const reg = Pci.BAR0 + bar * 4
    const command = Pci.read(address, Pci.COMMAND) & 0xFFFF
    Pci.write(address, Pci.COMMAND, command & @intCast(usize, 0xFFFF ^ Pci.MEMORY_SPACE))
    const original = Pci.read(address, reg)
    Pci.write(address, reg, 0xFFFFFFFF)
    var mask = (Pci.read(address, reg) & 0xFFFFFFF0) | 0xFFFFFFFF00000000
    Pci.write(address, reg, original)
    if original & 0x7 == 0x4 {
        const high = Pci.read(address, reg + 4)
        Pci.write(address, reg + 4, 0xFFFFFFFF)
        mask = (mask & 0xFFFFFFFF) | (Pci.read(address, reg + 4) << 32)
        Pci.write(address, reg + 4, high)
    }
    Pci.write(address, Pci.COMMAND, command)
    if mask & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF0 == 0 || mask == 0xFFFFFFFF00000000 { return 0 }
    return ~mask + 1
}

// How much of the VRAM aperture is put in the kernel's view (a card's
// aperture is 256 MiB unless it was made larger), and how much memory is
// set aside for what the card reads and writes.
const VRAM_VIEW: usize = 256 * 1024 * 1024
const DMA_PAGES: usize = 2048

// `bytes` of memory for the card (a ring, a page it writes back to), whole
// pages, zeroed: its physical address (what the card is told; the kernel
// sees it at layout.Memory.virtual of that), 0 when the piece set aside is
// used up. Never given back.
pub fn dma(bytes: usize) -> usize {
    const size = Page.up(bytes)
    if gpu.dmaBase == 0 || size == 0 || gpu.dmaUsed + size > DMA_PAGES * Page.SIZE { return 0 }
    const at = gpu.dmaBase + gpu.dmaUsed
    gpu.dmaUsed += size
    return at
}

// The card's message-signalled interrupt (trap.mlx, on layout.Vector.GPU).
pub fn interrupted() -> void { gpu.interrupts += 1 }

// The display controller as the firmware left it (stage 2b), for the next
// stage to start from.
var shown: scanout.Readout = undefined // (per run)

// The card as gpu_display.mlx reaches it: a register read in the aperture
// (one beyond it reads as all ones: the index and data window would be a
// write), the clock, the log.
fn displayRead(number: usize) -> u32 {
    if (number << 2) + 4 > gpu.registersSize { return 0xFFFFFFFF }
    return gpu.at(number << 2).*
}

fn displayNow() -> usize { return clock.system().monotonic() }

fn displayWrite(text: []const u8) -> void { console.out.write(text) }

// What this boot does with the display (../display_choice.mlx): worked
// out once from what the EFI loader handed over (the key it took at this
// start, what the machine remembers: layout.Info.CHOICE; 0 from a loader
// that knows nothing of it and under Multiboot) and the image's file
// settings/gpu (the boot archive's, whatever / is mounted from: the
// image that was started says it). `native`: the choice lets the driver
// write the display's registers and run the card's tables (the
// monitor's own mode, or the firmware's own set again); `wish`: which
// of the two the plan is made for.
var chosen: choice.Decision = undefined // (per run)
var chosenSaid = false // (per run)
var native = false // (per run)
var wish = mode.Wish.own // (per run)

fn readChoice() -> void {
    const file = archive.Archive.boot().find("settings/gpu")
    var text: []const u8 = ""
    if file.size != 0 { text = file.data[0..file.size] }
    chosen = choice.decide(cpu.BootInfo.get(layout.Info.CHOICE), text)
    chosenSaid = false
    native = false
    wish = if chosen.choice == choice.Choice.rehearsal { mode.Wish.rehearsal } else { mode.Wish.own }
}

// The choice said: on the screen for a card of ours (`card`), in the log
// alone when there is none for it to count on; how long the loader
// looked for a key in the log alone either way.
fn sayChoice(card: bool) -> void {
    chosenSaid = true
    const was = console.screen(card)
    choice.say(displayWrite, &chosen, card)
    _ = console.screen(false)
    choice.sayWait(displayWrite, &chosen, clock.system().nanoseconds(chosen.ticks) / 1000000)
    _ = console.screen(was)
}

// A register of the display written: the one place, and only at a boot
// whose choice lets the driver write (`native`). gpu_set.mlx is lent
// this and `displayClear`, nothing else of the card. (The I2C engine's
// and the DDC pins' words have a place of their own, `ddcWriteRegister`,
// with a condition of its own.)
fn displayWriteRegister(number: usize, value: u32) -> void {
    if !native || (number << 2) + 4 > gpu.registersSize { return }
    gpu.at(number << 2).* = value
}

// A part of the aperture set to black (a new picture's memory, before the
// controller shows it); never the part the console draws on.
fn displayClear(offset: usize, bytes: usize) -> void {
    if !native || gpu.vramView == 0 || offset + bytes > gpu.vramViewSize { return }
    if offset < gpu.consoleOffset + gpu.consoleBytes && offset + bytes > gpu.consoleOffset { return }
    Bytes.fill(Bytes.pointer(gpu.vramView + offset), bytes, 0)
}

// What the read-out was told of the aperture and the firmware's picture,
// the plan for the monitor's own mode, and what became of it.
var known: scanout.Memory = undefined // (per run)
var planned: mode.Plan = undefined // (per run)
var outcome: modeset.Result = undefined // (per run)

// The display controller read out and said: decoded, with what it shows
// and what the next stage would set, on the screen too; the registers
// themselves in the log alone (/devices/klog), where a wrong number can be
// looked for.
fn readDisplay() -> void {
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    knowPicture()
    scanout.report(&card, &known, &shown)
    const was = console.screen(false)
    scanout.dump(&card, &shown)
    _ = console.screen(was)
}

// The plan for the monitor's own mode (or, when this boot's choice is
// that, for the firmware's own mode set again), made from the read-out, the
// monitor's timing and, for a TMDS output, the card's ROM, and said:
// what it comes to on the screen too, its steps in the log alone.
// Nothing is written for it, and no table is run.
fn planDisplay() -> void {
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    if monitor.state == edid.State.none { console.out.write("gpu: the monitor's own timing is not known (no EDID from the firmware)\n") }
    var facts = mode.Rom.{ .machine = null, .board = null, .firmware = null }
    if tablesOpen {
        facts.machine = &machine
        facts.board = &board
        facts.firmware = &firmware
    }
    // (What the output's monitor said, when it was asked.)
    var said: ?*const dplink.Heard = null
    if asked { said = &heard }
    // (The monitor on another output than the firmware's, at a boot that
    // follows it: the plan brings the picture there, a new one of the
    // monitor's own size.)
    if decided.act == rule.Act.move && following.how == rule.How.follows {
        const target = rule.targetFor(&watch.ports, &following, decided, false)
        mode.makeTo(&card, &shown, &known, &target, &monitor, &offered, &facts, &planned)
    } else {
        mode.makeWith(&card, &shown, &known, &monitor, &offered, &facts, wish, said, &planned)
    }
    // (What the monitor says of HDMI matters to a TMDS output alone.)
    if planned.situation == mode.Situation.tmds { edid.sayOffer(displayWrite, &monitor, &offered) }
    mode.say(&card, &planned)
    const was = console.screen(false)
    mode.list(&card, &planned)
    _ = console.screen(was)
}

// The kernel's display whose picture is in this card's memory. A machine
// may have several displays (display.mlx: a board's own adapter beside
// this card), and the main one may be another adapter's: what this
// driver reads, plans for and retakes is the one in its own aperture
// (the card's first, should the firmware have given it several), not
// "the" display. One that is not present when the card shows none of
// the kernel's.
fn ownDisplay() -> *display.Display { return display.inMemory(gpu.vram, gpu.vramSize) }

// A picture in the card's aperture (`offset` bytes into it, `width` by
// `height`, `pitch` pixels from row to row) taken as the kernel's display,
// when the controller shows it: the display's record is the new picture's
// (display.mlx `retake`; what /devices/framebuffer tells and maps from
// here on, and the node's size), the console's part of the aperture is
// the new one, and the console is made anew on it (cleared, and what the
// screen showed drawn again). What the kernel says in between goes to the
// log and is drawn with the rest.
fn takePicture(offset: usize, width: usize, height: usize, pitch: usize) -> void {
    const screen = ownDisplay()
    // (No display of the loader's is in this card's memory: there is no
    // record to make the new picture's, and nothing to draw a console on.)
    if !screen.*.present { return }
    screen.retake(gpu.vram + offset, width, height, pitch * 4)
    gpu.consoleOffset = offset
    gpu.consoleBytes = screen.size()
    // (The node's size was the firmware's picture's when the file system
    // was made.)
    const tree = vfs.fileSystem()
    const devices = tree.lookup(vfs.Vfs.ROOT, "devices".pointer, 7)
    if devices != vfs.Vfs.NONE {
        const node = tree.lookup(devices, screen.nodeName().pointer, screen.nodeName().length)
        if node != vfs.Vfs.NONE { tree.node(node).*.size = screen.size() }
    }
    console.out.write("brixOS: display ")
    console.out.decimal(screen.*.width)
    console.out.write("x")
    console.out.decimal(screen.*.height)
    console.out.write(" pitch ")
    console.out.decimal(screen.*.pitch)
    console.out.write(" format ")
    console.out.decimal(screen.*.format)
    console.out.write(" at ")
    console.out.hex(screen.*.address)
    console.out.write(" (the monitor's own mode, set by the GPU's driver)\n")
    _ = console.reshapeFramebuffer()
}

// The plan carried out unless this boot's choice keeps the firmware's
// picture (gpu_set.mlx says what happens, and puts the firmware's state
// back when it fails). When the
// controller runs the new picture, it is the kernel's display from here
// on (`takePicture`), and the programs that map /devices/framebuffer
// later get the new picture and its size. No program runs yet, and the
// other processors are not started: nothing else draws while it changes.
fn setDisplay() -> void {
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    const hands = modeset.Hands.{ .write = displayWriteRegister, .clear = displayClear }
    native = choice.writes(chosen.choice)
    // (Nothing written yet, whichever way this ends: `beginFollowing`
    // reads it.)
    outcome.outcome = modeset.Outcome.untouched
    // (The choice "ask": the monitor was asked, the plan is said, and
    // that is all.)
    if chosen.choice == choice.Choice.ask {
        console.out.write("gpu: the monitor only asked at this boot: the plan is not carried out, nothing is written to the card but its AUX channel's own words, and none of its tables is run\n")
        return
    }
    // (No mode is set on an output whose hot plug line says that nothing
    // is plugged in: the one a move leaves has nothing, which is why it
    // is left.)
    if native && planned.complete && !planned.move.on && nobodyThere() {
        native = false
        return
    }
    // The new picture is cleared before the controller shows it: its
    // memory has to be in the kernel's view (`displayClear` leaves alone
    // what is not). (The firmware's own mode set again has no new picture
    // and clears nothing.)
    if native && planned.complete && !planned.rehearsal && (gpu.vramView == 0 || planned.offset + planned.bytes > gpu.vramViewSize) {
        native = false
        console.out.write("gpu: the monitor's own mode is not set, the firmware's picture stays: the new picture's memory is not in the kernel's view\n")
        return
    }
    // The card's tables, for a plan that runs some (they take a write
    // under the same condition as `displayWriteRegister`).
    var done = false
    modeSet = false
    outcome.outcome = modeset.Outcome.untouched
    if tablesOpen {
        const lent = modeset.Tables.{ .machine = &machine, .hands = tableHands(true), .aside = displayAside }
        var lentChannel: ?*aux.Channel = null
        if asked { lentChannel = &channel }
        done = modeset.attemptLink(&card, &hands, &lent, lentChannel, &planned, native, &outcome)
    } else {
        done = modeset.attempt(&card, &hands, &planned, native, &outcome)
    }
    // (A mode that did not come: `leaveChannel` gives the AUX channel's
    // words back.)
    if !done { return }
    modeSet = true
    // (The firmware's own mode set again, or a picture that already had
    // the monitor's size: the picture is where it was.)
    if planned.rehearsal || planned.move.keep { return }
    takePicture(planned.offset, planned.width, planned.height, planned.pitch)
}

// What the read-out and the plans are told of the kernel's picture in
// this card's memory: where it is and its size (the firmware's at first,
// the one a mode set made after it).
fn knowPicture() -> void {
    known = scanout.Memory.{
        .aperture = displayAperture(),
        .pictureOffset = 0,
        .pictureBytes = 0,
        .pictureWidth = 0,
        .pictureHeight = 0,
        .picturePitch = 0,
    }
    if gpu.consoleBytes == 0 { return }
    const screen = ownDisplay()
    known.pictureOffset = gpu.consoleOffset
    known.pictureBytes = gpu.consoleBytes
    known.pictureWidth = screen.*.width
    known.pictureHeight = screen.*.height
    known.picturePitch = screen.*.pitch / 4
}

// ---- A DisplayPort output's monitor asked over its AUX channel.

// Whether the channel may be written at this boot (`auxAsking`: its
// choice lets a monitor be asked, and the output is one whose monitor is),
// the channel of the output the firmware's picture leaves by, whether the
// monitor was asked by it (`asked`: the plan is made with what it said),
// what it said, and whether a mode was set afterwards (`modeSet`: the
// link is the driver's then, and the channel stays as it was made ready).
var auxAsking = false // (per run)
var asked = false // (per run)
var modeSet = false // (per run)
var auxCard: scanout.Card = undefined // (per run)
var auxPort: aux.Port = undefined // (per run)
var channel: aux.Channel = undefined // (per run)
var heard: dplink.Heard = undefined // (per run)
// The monitor's EDID as it came over the channel (two blocks at most).
var auxEdid: [layout.Blob.EDID_ROOM]u8 = undefined // (per run)

// A register of the AUX channels written: their six blocks, the pads'
// two calibration words, the DDC lines' pin words; nothing else, and
// only at a boot whose choice lets the monitor be asked. gpu_aux.mlx is
// lent this and `displayRead`, nothing else of the card.
fn auxWriteRegister(number: usize, value: u32) -> void {
    if !auxAsking || (number << 2) + 4 > gpu.registersSize { return }
    const channels = number >= aux.AUX_CONTROL && number < aux.AUX_CONTROL + aux.AUX_OFFSETS[aux.CHANNELS - 1] + 0x1C
    const pads = number == aux.AUXP_IMPCAL || number == aux.AUXN_IMPCAL
    const pins = number >= aux.DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK && number < aux.DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK + aux.DDC_STEP * aux.CHANNELS && (number - aux.DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK) % aux.DDC_STEP == 0
    if !channels && !pads && !pins { return }
    gpu.at(number << 2).* = value
}

// The monitor of the one running controller's output asked, when that
// output sends DisplayPort and the ROM names its connector's AUX channel
// and hot plug line; what it says of itself, what it holds of the link,
// the back end's words a training would write over, and its EDID, said
// in the log. Why it is not asked, when it is not, in one line.
fn askMonitor() -> void {
    auxAsking = false
    asked = false
    modeSet = false
    heard = dplink.nothingHeard()
    if shown.on != 1 { return }
    var n: usize = 0
    while n < scanout.CONTROLLERS && !shown.controllers[n].on { n += 1 }
    const encoder = &shown.controllers[n].encoder
    if !encoder.*.found || !scanout.isDisplayPort(encoder.*.mode) {
        if chosen.choice == choice.Choice.ask { console.out.write("gpu: there is nothing to ask: the picture does not leave by a DisplayPort output\n") }
        return
    }
    if !choice.asks(chosen.choice) {
        console.out.write("gpu: the monitor is not asked over its AUX channel: this boot keeps the firmware's picture and writes nothing\n")
        return
    }
    if !tablesOpen || !board.found {
        console.out.write("gpu: the monitor is not asked over its AUX channel: the card's ROM, which names the output's channel, was not read or does not hold\n")
        return
    }
    const which = board.byBlock(encoder.*.backEnd)
    if which == videorom.NONE || board.connectors[which].kind != videorom.CONNECTOR_OBJECT_ID_DISPLAYPORT || board.connectors[which].aux == videorom.NONE || board.connectors[which].hotPlugLine == videorom.NONE || board.connectors[which].hotPlugLine == 0 {
        console.out.write("gpu: the monitor is not asked over its AUX channel: the ROM names no DisplayPort connector with an AUX channel and a hot plug line behind DIG ")
        console.out.decimal(encoder.*.backEnd)
        console.out.write("\n")
        return
    }
    const connector = &board.connectors[which]
    auxAsking = true
    auxCard = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    auxPort = aux.Port.{ .card = &auxCard, .write = auxWriteRegister }
    channel = aux.Channel.open(&auxPort, connector.*.aux, connector.*.hotPlugLine - 1)
    dplink.ask(&channel, &heard)
    asked = true
    if heard.state == dplink.Asked.heard {
        var words: [dplink.WORDS]usize = undefined
        const was = console.screen(false)
        dplink.words(&auxCard, encoder.*.backEnd, &words)
        dplink.sayWords(&auxCard, encoder.*.backEnd, &words, "as the firmware left it")
        _ = console.screen(was)
        readAuxEdid()
    }
}

// The AUX channel's words given back as the asking found them (the hot
// plug line it watches, its receive window, its pins), unless a mode was
// set at this boot: after the asking alone, after a plan that is not
// complete or was not carried out, after a change that failed and was
// put back.
fn leaveChannel() -> void {
    if asked && !modeSet { channel.end() }
}

// The monitor's EDID read through the AUX channel's I2C bridge (its
// first block, and its second when it names one). It is the EDID of the
// monitor on this output; the loader's is the one of the firmware's
// display, which need not be this one (two adapters, a card that is not
// the firmware's console). When it gives a timing and that is another
// than the loader's gave, or the loader passed none on, it counts.
fn readAuxEdid() -> void {
    if !channel.edid(0, auxEdid[0..aux.EDID_LENGTH]) {
        console.out.write("gpu: the monitor's EDID was not read over its AUX channel: the one the loader passed on stays\n")
        return
    }
    var count = aux.EDID_LENGTH
    if auxEdid[126] != 0 && 2 * aux.EDID_LENGTH <= layout.Blob.EDID_ROOM && channel.edid(1, auxEdid[aux.EDID_LENGTH..2 * aux.EDID_LENGTH]) { count = 2 * aux.EDID_LENGTH }
    const own = edid.read(auxEdid[0..count])
    if own.state != edid.State.found {
        console.out.write("gpu: the monitor's EDID as read over its AUX channel gives no timing: the one the loader passed on stays\n")
        return
    }
    const t = &own.timing
    const l = &monitor.timing
    if monitor.state == edid.State.found && t.*.clock == l.*.clock && t.*.width == l.*.width && t.*.height == l.*.height && t.*.hTotal == l.*.hTotal && t.*.vTotal == l.*.vTotal {
        console.out.write("gpu: the monitor's EDID over its AUX channel gives the timing the loader's did\n")
        return
    }
    console.out.write(if monitor.state == edid.State.found { "gpu: the monitor's EDID over its AUX channel gives another timing than the one the loader passed on: it is this output's monitor, so it counts\n" } else { "gpu: the monitor's EDID was read over its AUX channel (the loader passed none on)\n" })
    monitor = own
    offered = edid.offer(auxEdid[0..count])
    edid.say(displayWrite, &monitor)
}

// What the driver says from here on is for the log alone (while a mode
// is being set the screen is dark, and what each table did is many
// lines), or for the screen too again.
var asideWas = true // (per run)

fn displayAside(on: bool) -> void {
    if on {
        asideWas = console.screen(false)
    } else {
        _ = console.screen(asideWas)
    }
}

// The card's video BIOS ROM (gpu_rom.mlx): where the card is on the PCI
// bus, the image's bytes in the kernel's view (videorom.ROOM of them; 0:
// none), what was found, and the ROM's tables: the interpreter with the
// ROM open, its scratch (64 KiB at most), the clocks and the connectors.
var romDevice: usize = 0 // (per run)
var romBytes: usize = 0 // (per run)
var rom: videorom.Rom = undefined // (per run)
var machine: atom.Atom = undefined // (per run)
var tablesOpen = false // (per run)
var firmware: videorom.Firmware = undefined // (per run)
var board: videorom.Board = undefined // (per run)
const SCRATCH_WORDS: usize = 16384
var scratch: [SCRATCH_WORDS]u32 = undefined // (per run)

// The card as gpu_rom.mlx reads the ROM from it. These are the writes
// every boot makes to read the ROM, one that keeps the firmware's picture
// too (the file's head; gpu_rom.mlx puts each word back).
fn romConfig(offset: usize) -> usize { return Pci.read(romDevice, offset) }

fn romSetConfig(offset: usize, value: usize) -> void { Pci.write(romDevice, offset, value) }

// Whether the card's ROM may be switched on at `address` (gpu_rom.mlx's
// `mayAnswer`): inside the memory window of the bridge whose secondary
// bus is the card's (a card on bus 0 has none: its ROM is not read this
// way), and on none of the card's own memories (the registers, the VRAM
// aperture, the doorbells, and the first memory of the card's second
// function, its HDMI audio: of a memory whose size is not known, as much
// as its address's lowest set bit allows).
fn romClear(address: usize, size: usize) -> bool {
    const bus = (romDevice >> 16) & 0xFF
    var window = videorom.Stretch.{ .from = 0, .bytes = 0 }
    var found = false
    var at: usize = 0
    while at < 256 && !found && bus != 0 {
        var slot: usize = 0
        while slot < 32 && !found {
            var function: usize = 0
            while function < 8 && !found {
                const bridge = Pci.atBus(at, slot, function)
                const id = Pci.read(bridge, Pci.VENDOR_DEVICE)
                const header = if id & 0xFFFF != 0xFFFF { Pci.read(bridge, Pci.HEADER_TYPE) } else { 0 }
                // (Header type 1: a PCI bridge.)
                if id & 0xFFFF != 0xFFFF && (header >> 16) & 0x7F == 1 && (Pci.read(bridge, Pci.SECONDARY_BUS) >> 8) & 0xFF == bus {
                    window = videorom.windowOf(Pci.read(bridge, videorom.PCI_MEMORY_BASE))
                    found = true
                }
                if function == 0 && (id & 0xFFFF == 0xFFFF || header & 0x800000 == 0) { break }
                function += 1
            }
            slot += 1
        }
        at += 1
    }
    const audio = Pci.memBase(romDevice | (1 << 8), 0)
    const taken = [_]videorom.Stretch{
        videorom.Stretch.{ .from = gpu.registersPhysical, .bytes = gpu.registersSize },
        videorom.Stretch.{ .from = gpu.vram, .bytes = gpu.vramSize },
        videorom.Stretch.{ .from = gpu.doorbells, .bytes = if gpu.doorbells != 0 { gpu.doorbells & (~gpu.doorbells + 1) } else { 0 } },
        videorom.Stretch.{ .from = audio, .bytes = if audio != 0 { audio & (~audio + 1) } else { 0 } }
    }
    return videorom.mayAnswer(address, size, &window, taken[0..])
}

fn romWindow(physical: usize, into: []u8) -> bool {
    const view = memory.Device.map(physical, into.length, memory.Device.UNCACHED)
    if view == 0 { return false }
    const bytes = unsafe { @ptrFromInt([*]const u8, view) }
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < into.length {
        into[k] = bytes[k]
        k += 1
    }
    return true
}

fn romRegister(number: usize) -> u32 {
    if (number << 2) + 4 > gpu.registersSize { return 0xFFFFFFFF }
    return gpu.at(number << 2).*
}

fn romSetRegister(number: usize, value: u32) -> void {
    if (number << 2) + 4 > gpu.registersSize { return }
    gpu.at(number << 2).* = value
}

// How many ROMs were put in /devices (a second card's is "gpurom1").
var romFiles: usize = 0 // (per run)

// The card's video BIOS ROM read (gpu_rom.mlx's `read`: the firmware's
// ACPI table, the PCI expansion ROM, the card's ROM registers), said, its
// bytes put where programs find them and, for a card the firmware has
// started (`started`), its tables made ready. On the screen: what the ROM
// is, and what is wrong with it; the tables, clocks and connectors in the
// log alone.
fn readRom(address: usize, started: bool) -> void {
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    const bus = videorom.Bus.{ .config = romConfig, .setConfig = romSetConfig, .clear = romClear, .window = romWindow, .read = romRegister, .write = romSetRegister }
    romDevice = address
    tablesOpen = false
    // Twice a ROM's most bytes: the image that is kept, and room to read
    // into.
    const pages = Page.up(2 * videorom.ROOM) / Page.SIZE
    const frames = memory.allFrames().allocateContiguous(pages)
    if frames == 0 {
        console.out.write("gpu: video BIOS not read (no memory for it)\n")
        return
    }
    romBytes = layout.Memory.virtual(frames)
    const image = Bytes.pointer(romBytes)[0..videorom.ROOM]
    const work = Bytes.pointer(romBytes + videorom.ROOM)[0..videorom.ROOM]
    // The firmware's table, when it has one (all of it is in the kernel's
    // view; nothing of the card is touched for it).
    const identity = Pci.read(address, 0)
    const place = videorom.Place.{ .bus = (address >> 16) & 0xFF, .device = (address >> 11) & 0x1F, .function = (address >> 8) & 0x7, .vendor = identity & 0xFFFF, .product = (identity >> 16) & 0xFFFF }
    const table = acpi.table(acpi.root(), "VFCT", 0)
    const length = if table != 0 { acpi.lengthOf(table) } else { 0 }
    const vfct = Bytes.pointer(if table != 0 { table } else { romBytes })[0..length]
    videorom.read(vfct, &place, &bus, gpu.registers != 0 && gpu.registersSize != 0, image, work, &rom)
    videorom.sayRom(&card, &rom)
    if !rom.kept() { return }
    if romFiles < 2 && vfs.fileSystem().addDeviceBytes(if romFiles == 0 { "gpurom" } else { "gpurom1" }, image.pointer, rom.size) {
        romFiles += 1
    } else {
        console.out.write("gpu: video BIOS: no file of it could be made in /devices\n")
    }
    if !rom.sound() { return }
    if machine.open(image[0..rom.size], scratch[0..]) != atom.Opened.ok {
        console.out.write("gpu: video BIOS: its lists of tables do not lie inside it: none of its tables is run\n")
        return
    }
    // The scratch the ROM asks for, as far as there is room.
    const asked = videorom.scratchBytes(&machine) / 4
    const words = if asked < SCRATCH_WORDS { asked } else { SCRATCH_WORDS }
    machine.scratch = scratch[0..words]
    videorom.readFirmware(&machine, &firmware)
    videorom.readBoard(&machine, &board)
    const was = console.screen(false)
    const there = videorom.sayTables(&card, &machine)
    if asked > SCRATCH_WORDS {
        card.field("gpu: video BIOS: its tables ask for ", asked * 4 / 1024)
        card.field(" KiB of scratch, they are given ", SCRATCH_WORDS * 4 / 1024)
        card.say("\n")
    }
    videorom.sayFirmware(&card, &firmware)
    videorom.sayBoard(&card, &board)
    _ = console.screen(was)
    if there != videorom.neededTables() {
        card.field("gpu: video BIOS: only ", there)
        card.field(" of the ", videorom.neededTables())
        card.say(" tables a mode set runs are in it\n")
    }
    // ASIC_Init starts the card anew: it is never run, whoever asks.
    machine.never = videorom.Command.ASIC_Init
    // A card the firmware has not started needs just that table run
    // first: none of its tables is lent.
    if !started {
        console.out.write("gpu: video BIOS: the card is not started, so none of its tables is run\n")
        return
    }
    tablesOpen = true
}

// Which of the ROM's connectors each running controller goes out by (the
// read-out's DIG block is the transmitter's number).
fn sayOutputs() -> void {
    if !tablesOpen || !board.found { return }
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    var n: usize = 0
    while n < scanout.CONTROLLERS {
        const controller = &shown.controllers[n]
        if controller.*.on && controller.*.encoder.found {
            const which = board.byBlock(controller.*.encoder.backEnd)
            card.controller(n)
            if which == videorom.NONE {
                card.field(" goes out by DIG ", controller.*.encoder.backEnd)
                card.say(", which is none of the ROM's connectors\n")
            } else {
                card.field(" goes out by the ROM's connector ", which)
                card.say(": ")
                videorom.sayConnector(&card, &board.connectors[which])
                card.say("\n")
            }
        }
        n += 1
    }
}

// Where the monitor is (gpu_plug.mlx): every connector's record, the
// reading of an EDID that is under way, and what was detected at boot.
// `watching`: there is a card whose connectors are looked at from the
// timer's tick; `asking`: this boot's choice lets a monitor be asked
// over its DDC line (every choice but the one that keeps the firmware's
// picture and writes nothing).
var watch: plug.Watch = undefined // (per run)
var watching = false // (per run)
var asking = false // (per run)
var verdict: plug.Verdict = undefined // (per run)

// A word of the card's I2C engine or of a DDC line's pins written
// (gpu_ddc.mlx's hands): those and no other register, and none at a boot
// that keeps the firmware's picture.
fn ddcWriteRegister(number: usize, value: u32) -> void {
    if !asking || (number << 2) + 4 > gpu.registersSize { return }
    const engine = number >= ddc.DC_I2C_CONTROL && number <= ddc.DC_I2C_DATA
    const pins = number >= ddc.DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK && number < ddc.DC_GPIO_DDC1_MASK + 4 * ddc.LINES
    if !engine && !pins { return }
    gpu.at(number << 2).* = value
}

fn ddcPause(microseconds: usize) -> void { clock.pause(microseconds) }

// How many DDC lines the card has: six on Polaris 10, five on Polaris 11
// and 12 (Linux's dce112_resource.c, by the chip).
fn ddcLines() -> usize { return if (gpu.device >= 0x67C0 && gpu.device <= 0x67DF) || gpu.device == 0x6FDF { ddc.LINES } else { ddc.LINES_SMALL } }

// Every connector looked at, each said, and what was detected: where the
// monitor is and which path follows. Registers are read for it, none
// written. The lines' own words go to the log alone.
fn lookAtConnectors() -> void {
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    if tablesOpen {
        plug.survey(&card, &machine, &board, &watch.ports)
    } else {
        plug.survey(&card, null, null, &watch.ports)
    }
    watch.begin(&card, ddcLines(), if firmware.found { firmware.reference } else { 0 })
    plug.sayPorts(&card, &watch.ports)
    const was = console.screen(false)
    plug.sayWords(&card, &watch.ports)
    _ = console.screen(was)
    plug.detect(&watch.ports, &verdict)
    plug.sayVerdict(&card, &watch.ports, &verdict)
}

// The EDID the plan's monitor was read from, the loader's (for the
// driver's own reading to be held against): where its bytes lie in the
// kernel's view, and how many.
var loaderEdidAt: usize = 0 // (per run)
var loaderEdidBytes: usize = 0 // (per run)

// The monitors asked over their DDC lines (gpu_plug.mlx's `askAtBoot`
// says which), unless this boot keeps the firmware's picture. The EDID
// read from the connector the kernel's picture goes out by is held
// against the one the loader passed on, and gpu_plug.mlx's `counts`
// says which of the two the plan is made from: the driver's own
// whenever the firmware gave the loader fewer blocks than the monitor
// has, or none, or another monitor's; the loader's when the driver's
// reading failed or gave less.
fn askMonitors() -> void {
    asking = chosen.choice != choice.Choice.firmware
    if !watch.ports.found { return }
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    if !asking {
        var k: usize = 0
        while k < watch.ports.count {
            if plug.askedAtBoot(&watch.ports.ports[k]) {
                console.out.write("gpu: no monitor is asked for its EDID at this boot: it keeps the firmware's picture and writes nothing to the card\n")
                return
            }
            k += 1
        }
        return
    }
    const hands = ddc.Hands.{ .write = ddcWriteRegister }
    plug.askAtBoot(&card, &hands, ddcPause, watch.lines, watch.crystal, &watch.ports, &watch.reader)
    // (Another adapter shows the kernel's picture: the loader's EDID is
    // that adapter's monitor's, and no plan is made for this card.)
    if gpu.consoleBytes == 0 || verdict.outputs != 1 { return }
    const port = &watch.ports.ports[verdict.output]
    if port.*.heard.outcome == ddc.Outcome.not_asked { return }
    var at: usize = 0
    const loader = unsafe { @ptrFromInt([*]const u8, loaderEdidAt) }[0..loaderEdidBytes]
    const which = plug.counts(&port.*.heard, loader, &at)
    plug.sayCounts(&card, port, which, at, loaderEdidBytes)
    if !plug.fromConnector(which) { return }
    monitor = port.*.monitor
    offered = port.*.offer
    // (The monitor's own timing said anew where it is read from other
    // bytes than before.)
    if which != plug.Counts.same { edid.say(displayWrite, &monitor) }
}

// Whether the plan's output has, for certain, nothing plugged in
// (gpu_plug.mlx's `emptyFor`): said, and then no mode is set there.
fn nobodyThere() -> bool {
    const empty = plug.emptyFor(&watch.ports, planned.controller)
    if empty == plug.NONE { return false }
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    console.out.write(if planned.rehearsal { "gpu: the firmware's own mode is not set again: nothing is plugged into " } else { "gpu: the monitor's own mode is not set: nothing is plugged into " })
    plug.sayName(&card, &watch.ports.ports[empty])
    console.out.write(", where the picture goes out (its hot plug line is low; Linux sets no mode on such an output either)\n")
    return true
}

// The monitor followed (gpu_switch.mlx): how far this boot's choice lets
// the rule go, what it decided at boot, and its record. What a change
// while brixOS runs is carried out with is the one driver's
// (gpu_together.mlx; `joinUp` at the end of this file).
var following: rule.Follow = undefined // (per run)
var decided: rule.Decision = undefined // (per run)

// "firmware" asks nobody and writes nothing; a choice that does not set
// the monitor's own mode asks and does not follow; the default follows.
fn howOf() -> rule.How {
    if chosen.choice == choice.Choice.firmware { return rule.How.quiet }
    if !choice.writes(chosen.choice) || wish == mode.Wish.rehearsal { return rule.How.says }
    return rule.How.follows
}

// The rule at boot, after the monitors were asked: where the monitor is
// against where the firmware's picture goes out, said; for a move the
// plan's monitor is the one on the connector the picture is brought to
// (its EDID as read there: there is no other).
fn decideAtBoot() -> void {
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    decided = rule.atBoot(&card, &watch.ports, &verdict, &following, howOf())
    if decided.act != rule.Act.move || following.how != rule.How.follows { return }
    monitor = watch.ports.ports[decided.to].monitor
    offered = watch.ports.ports[decided.to].offer
}

// The record begun once the boot's own change is over. From here on the
// driver writes the display only for a change the rule decides, and only
// at a boot that follows the monitor (`native`), or for a mode a program
// asks for (`modeSetTo`).
fn beginFollowing() -> void {
    knowPicture()
    var controller: usize = 0
    while controller < scanout.CONTROLLERS && !shown.controllers[controller].on { controller += 1 }
    if planned.situation != mode.Situation.none { controller = planned.controller }
    var bytes = unsafe { @ptrFromInt([*]const u8, loaderEdidAt) }[0..loaderEdidBytes]
    const s = plug.servedBy(&watch.ports, controller)
    if s != plug.NONE && watch.ports.ports[s].heard.outcome == ddc.Outcome.read { bytes = watch.ports.ports[s].heard.data() }
    rule.begin(&following, following.how, &watch.ports, controller, known.pictureWidth, known.pictureHeight, bytes, 0, 0, 0)
    const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    _ = rule.carried(&card, &following, &watch.ports, &planned, &outcome, decided)
    native = following.how == rule.How.follows
}

// The timer's tick (trap.mlx, in its interrupt): the connectors looked at
// when a look is due, a change said, an EDID's reading taken a piece
// further; then the rule's turn, whose change is made after the
// interrupt (gpu_together.mlx). Nothing on a machine without a card
// whose connectors are known.
pub fn tick() -> void {
    if !watching || !joined { return }
    together.tick(&driver)
}

// Every connector's record, as it stands now (gpu_plug.mlx's `Ports`;
// `found` false: there is none), and what the last tick saw change.
pub fn connectors() -> *const plug.Ports { return &watch.ports }

pub fn changes() -> *const plug.Changes { return &watch.changes }

// The card as a ROM's table reaches it (atom.mlx's Hands): the registers
// in the aperture; the PLL's and the memory controller's registers as
// Linux's amdgpu has them for the tables (reads of 0, writes that do
// nothing: cail_pll_read and the three beside it); waits; the clock. A
// register is written only at a boot whose choice lets the driver write
// (`native`).
fn tableRead(number: usize) -> u32 { return gpu.at(number << 2).* }

fn tableWrite(number: usize, value: u32) -> bool {
    if !native { return false }
    gpu.at(number << 2).* = value
    return true
}

fn tableWriteRefused(number: usize, value: u32) -> bool { return false }

fn tableOtherRead(number: usize) -> u32 { return 0 }

fn tableOtherWrite(number: usize, value: u32) -> bool { return true }

fn tableDelay(microseconds: usize) -> void { clock.pause(microseconds) }

// The interpreter with the card's ROM open; null when there is no ROM
// that holds.
pub fn tables() -> ?*atom.Atom {
    if tablesOpen { return &machine }
    return null
}

// The card for a table's run: `writing` false gives hands that take no
// write (a run stops at its first and says what it would have written).
pub fn tableHands(writing: bool) -> atom.Hands {
    var hands = atom.Hands.{ .registers = gpu.registersSize / 4, .read = tableRead, .write = tableWriteRefused, .pllRead = tableOtherRead, .pllWrite = tableOtherWrite, .mcRead = tableOtherRead, .mcWrite = tableOtherWrite, .delay = tableDelay, .now = displayNow }
    if writing { hands.write = tableWrite }
    return hands
}

// The ROM's clocks and connectors (found: false when they were not
// read).
pub fn firmwareInformation() -> *const videorom.Firmware { return &firmware }

pub fn outputs() -> *const videorom.Board { return &board }

// What the monitor of the firmware's display says of itself: its EDID as
// the EFI loader passed it on (layout.Blob.EDID, Info.EDID_BYTES bytes of
// it; none under a Multiboot loader), read for the monitor's own timing
// and said in the log. Whatever card there is, or none: the timing is the
// monitor's.
var monitor: edid.Monitor = undefined // (per run)
// What else the EDID offers: the other timings of the monitor's own size
// and what it says of HDMI (a TMDS output's plan chooses by them).
var offered: edid.Offer = undefined // (per run)

fn readMonitor() -> void {
    var count = cpu.BootInfo.get(layout.Info.EDID_BYTES)
    if count > layout.Blob.EDID_ROOM { count = layout.Blob.EDID_ROOM }
    var bytes = unsafe { @ptrFromInt([*]const u8, layout.Blob.virtual(layout.Blob.EDID)) }
    if count == 0 {
        // A virtual machine's Bochs adapter (QEMU's VGA and bochs-display)
        // keeps its monitor's EDID at the start of its register area, and
        // its firmware passes none on: taken from there when it begins as
        // an EDID does.
        const adapter = bochsEdid()
        if adapter != 0 {
            bytes = unsafe { @ptrFromInt([*]const u8, adapter) }
            count = layout.Blob.EDID_ROOM
        }
    }
    monitor = edid.read(bytes[0..count])
    offered = edid.offer(bytes[0..count])
    loaderEdidAt = @intFromPtr(bytes)
    loaderEdidBytes = count
    edid.say(displayWrite, &monitor)
}

// The Bochs display adapter's EDID (PCI 1234:1111; the first bytes of
// the memory its BAR2 names: "edid data blob", QEMU's
// docs/specs/standard-vga), in the kernel's view; 0 when there is no such
// adapter or the bytes do not begin as an EDID.
fn bochsEdid() -> usize {
    const adapter = Pci.find(0x1234, 0x1111)
    if adapter == Pci.NONE { return 0 }
    const physical = Pci.memBase(adapter, 2)
    if physical == 0 { return 0 }
    const view = memory.Device.map(physical, 4096, memory.Device.UNCACHED)
    if view == 0 { return 0 }
    const bytes = unsafe { @ptrFromInt([*]const u8, view) }
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < 8 {
        const wanted: u8 = if k == 0 || k == 7 { 0 } else { 0xFF }
        if bytes[k] != wanted { return 0 }
        k += 1
    }
    return view
}

// The AMD display device number `n` of class `code` (VGA, or another
// display's); Pci.NONE when there are no more of the class.
fn candidate(code: usize, n: usize) -> usize { return Pci.findClass(code, 0xFFFF00, n) }

// The card at `address` taken: enabled, its apertures recorded and put in
// the kernel's view, its register path tried; whether it answers.
fn take(address: usize) -> bool {
    Pci.enableMemory(address)
    gpu.device = (Pci.read(address, 0) >> 16) & 0xFFFF
    gpu.revision = Pci.read(address, Pci.CLASS) & 0xFF
    gpu.vram = Pci.memBase(address, 0)
    gpu.vramSize = barSize(address, 0)
    gpu.doorbells = Pci.memBase(address, 2)
    gpu.registersPhysical = Pci.memBase(address, 5)
    gpu.registersSize = barSize(address, 5)
    gpu.vramView = 0
    gpu.vramViewSize = 0
    gpu.doorbellView = 0
    gpu.consoleOffset = 0
    gpu.consoleBytes = 0
    gpu.dmaBase = 0
    gpu.dmaUsed = 0
    gpu.interrupts = 0
    if gpu.registersPhysical == 0 || gpu.registersSize == 0 { return false }
    gpu.registers = memory.Device.map(gpu.registersPhysical, gpu.registersSize, memory.Device.UNCACHED)
    if gpu.registers == 0 { return false }
    if !gpu.posted() {
        // A card the firmware has not started (it is not the firmware's
        // display, and the firmware did not run its driver): nothing of
        // it is read or set but its ROM, which says what card it is.
        console.out.write("gpu: an AMD display device that the firmware has not started (its registers do not answer as a started card's): left alone, but for reading its ROM\n")
        firmware.found = false
        board.found = false
        if gpu.polaris() { readRom(address, false) }
        return false
    }
    // The VRAM aperture write-combining, as the console's part of it is
    // already (another caching there would change the console's pages
    // under it), the doorbells uncached.
    if gpu.vram != 0 && gpu.vramSize != 0 {
        gpu.vramViewSize = if gpu.vramSize > VRAM_VIEW { VRAM_VIEW } else { gpu.vramSize }
        gpu.vramView = memory.Device.map(gpu.vram, gpu.vramViewSize, memory.Device.WRITE_COMBINING)
        if gpu.vramView == 0 { gpu.vramViewSize = 0 }
    }
    if gpu.doorbells != 0 { gpu.doorbellView = memory.Device.map(gpu.doorbells, layout.Memory.HUGE, memory.Device.UNCACHED) }
    // The firmware's picture, when it is in this card's aperture.
    const screen = ownDisplay()
    if screen.*.present && screen.*.address >= gpu.vram && screen.*.address < gpu.vram + gpu.vramSize {
        gpu.consoleOffset = screen.*.address - gpu.vram
        gpu.consoleBytes = screen.size()
    }
    gpu.dmaBase = memory.allFrames().allocateContiguous(DMA_PAGES)
    gpu.present = true
    console.out.write("brixOS: gpu0, ")
    console.out.write(gpu.name())
    console.out.write(", ")
    console.out.decimal(gpu.vramSize / (1024 * 1024))
    console.out.write(" MiB VRAM aperture at ")
    console.out.hex(gpu.vram)
    console.out.write(", registers at ")
    console.out.hex(gpu.registersPhysical)
    console.out.write(", doorbells at ")
    console.out.hex(gpu.doorbells)
    console.out.write("\n")
    if gpu.consoleBytes != 0 {
        console.out.write("gpu: the firmware's picture is ")
        console.out.decimal(gpu.consoleBytes / 1024)
        console.out.write(" KiB at ")
        console.out.hex(gpu.consoleOffset)
        console.out.write(" of the aperture\n")
    } else if display.screen().*.present {
        // (Another adapter shows the firmware's picture, the board's own,
        // say, and no display the loader passed on is in this card's
        // memory. This card is started all the same.)
        console.out.write("gpu: the firmware's picture is not in this card's memory: another adapter shows it\n")
    } else {
        console.out.write("gpu: the firmware gave no picture\n")
    }
    gpu.report()
    firmware.found = false
    board.found = false
    // (The monitor read at the start is the one of the firmware's first
    // display. When this card's display is a further one, the monitor to
    // plan for is that display's: its EDID as the loader passed it on.)
    if screen.*.present && !screen.*.first {
        console.out.write("gpu: this card's display is not the firmware's first: its own monitor's EDID counts\n")
        monitor = edid.read(unsafe { @ptrFromInt([*]const u8, screen.*.edid) }[0..screen.*.edidBytes])
        offered = edid.offer(unsafe { @ptrFromInt([*]const u8, screen.*.edid) }[0..screen.*.edidBytes])
        loaderEdidAt = screen.*.edid
        loaderEdidBytes = screen.*.edidBytes
        edid.say(displayWrite, &monitor)
    }
    if gpu.polaris() {
        readRom(address, true)
        readDisplay()
        sayOutputs()
        lookAtConnectors()
        sayChoice(true)
        askMonitors()
        askMonitor()
        decideAtBoot()
        planDisplay()
        setDisplay()
        leaveChannel()
        // (What serves each connector read anew: a mode set may have
        // changed what the output sends. From here on the timer's tick
        // looks at the connectors, and the monitor is followed.)
        const card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
        plug.serveAll(&card, &watch.ports)
        beginFollowing()
        watching = watch.ports.found
        bringEngines(address)
        joinUp()
    } else {
        console.out.write("gpu: the display controller is not read (its registers are known for Polaris cards only)\n")
    }
    return true
}

// The first AMD GPU, enabled, its apertures recorded, its register path
// brought up; whether there is one. (A VGA-class PCI device whose vendor
// is AMD, else one of another display class.)
pub fn probe() -> bool {
    gpu.present = false
    gpu.registers = 0
    watching = false
    asking = false
    following.how = rule.How.off
    readMonitor()
    readChoice()
    const found = find()
    // (No card whose display this driver sets: the choice is said all
    // the same, in the log alone, with what the loader found.)
    if !chosenSaid { sayChoice(false) }
    if !engineSaid { enginesWithoutCard() }
    return found
}

fn find() -> bool {
    var pass: usize = 0
    while pass < 2 {
        var n: usize = 0
        while n < 64 {
            const address = candidate(if pass == 0 { 0x030000 } else { 0x038000 }, n)
            n += 1
            if address == Pci.NONE { break }
            if Pci.read(address, 0) & 0xFFFF != VENDOR_AMD { continue }
            if take(address) { return true }
        }
        pass += 1
    }
    return false
}

// ---- The card's engines: the first stages toward a card that takes
// commands (gpu_engines.mlx), as far as this boot's digit says
// (../engine_choice.mlx: a digit held while the machine starts, in the
// boot information's word Info.STAGE; none: stage 0, which reads and
// says and writes nothing but index words). They run once the display is
// final for this boot (`take`, after `leaveChannel` and the connectors'
// watch), for a Polaris 10 the firmware started, with the hot plug tick
// kept off the card. A boot that goes beyond stage 0 gives the display's
// plan the aperture less its top 64 MiB (`displayAperture`), the
// driver's part.

var engineState: engines.Engines = undefined // (per run)
var engineSaid = false // (per run)
var engineBegun = false // (per run)
var engineDevice: usize = 0 // (per run)
var engineWatching = false // (per run)

// How far this boot goes: the loader's digit, whether it had a keyboard,
// and the display's choice (F holds every stage back at 0).
fn engineDecision() -> stages.Decision { return stages.decide(cpu.BootInfo.get(layout.Info.STAGE), cpu.BootInfo.get(layout.Info.CHOICE), chosen.choice) }

// The part of the aperture the display's plan may use at this boot: all
// of the kernel's view at stage 0 (nothing changes for it), the view less
// the driver's top 64 MiB from stage 1 on, on a card whose stages run.
fn displayAperture() -> usize {
    const view = if gpu.vramViewSize != 0 { gpu.vramViewSize } else { gpu.vramSize }
    if !engines.polaris10(gpu.device) || gpu.vramViewSize == 0 { return view }
    const limit = engines.displayLimit(view, engineDecision().stage)
    return if limit == 0 { view } else { limit }
}

// The card as the steps reach it: its registers (gpu.rreg and wreg: all
// the steps' registers lie in the register aperture), the aperture's
// words in the kernel's view, the memory set aside for the card (`dma`;
// the kernel sees the system's memory at layout.Memory.virtual), the PCI
// command word, the interrupt as a message to the first processor, the
// firmware files of the boot archive.
fn engineRead(number: usize) -> u32 { return gpu.rreg(number) }

fn engineWrite(number: usize, value: u32) -> void { gpu.wreg(number, value) }

fn enginePause(microseconds: usize) -> void { clock.pause(microseconds) }

fn engineVramRead(offset: usize) -> usize {
    if gpu.vramView == 0 || offset + 8 > gpu.vramViewSize { return engines.NONE }
    return unsafe { @ptrFromInt(*usize, gpu.vramView + offset) }.*
}

fn engineVramWrite(offset: usize, value: usize) -> void {
    if gpu.vramView == 0 || offset + 8 > gpu.vramViewSize { return }
    const at = unsafe { @ptrFromInt(*usize, gpu.vramView + offset) }
    at.* = value
}

fn engineMemoryRead(physical: usize) -> u32 { return unsafe { @ptrFromInt(*u32, layout.Memory.virtual(physical)) }.* }

fn engineCommand() -> usize { return Pci.read(engineDevice, Pci.COMMAND) & 0xFFFF }

fn engineRoute() -> bool {
    const apic = smp.localApic()
    if !apic.0 || engineDevice == 0 { return false }
    return Pci.enableMessages(engineDevice, layout.Vector.GPU, apic.1) != 0
}

fn engineInterrupts() -> usize { return gpu.interrupts }

fn engineWaiting() -> bool { return smp.waiting(layout.Vector.GPU) }

// Stages 3 and 4 (gpu_smc_sdma.mlx) write the memory set aside for the
// card (rings, the firmware's copies) and ring the card's doorbells.
var loadedState: loaded.Loaded = undefined // (per run)

fn engineMemoryWrite(physical: usize, value: u32) -> void {
    const at = unsafe { @ptrFromInt(*u32, layout.Memory.virtual(physical)) }
    at.* = value
}

fn engineDoorbell(index: usize, value: u32) -> void {
    if gpu.doorbellView == 0 || (index + 1) * 4 > layout.Memory.HUGE { return }
    const at = unsafe { @ptrFromInt(*u32, gpu.doorbellView + index * 4) }
    at.* = value
}

var engineFileName: [64]u8 = undefined // (per run)

fn engineFile(name: []const u8) -> []const u8 {
    const prefix = "firmware/amdgpu/"
    if prefix.length + name.length > engineFileName.length { return name[0..0] }
    var k: usize = 0
    while k < prefix.length {
        engineFileName[k] = prefix[k]
        k += 1
    }
    var j: usize = 0
    while j < name.length {
        engineFileName[k + j] = name[j]
        j += 1
    }
    const file = archive.Archive.boot().find(engineFileName[0..k + j])
    if file.size == 0 { return name[0..0] }
    return file.data[0..file.size]
}

// The hot plug tick kept off the card for the steps (the reviewers' rule
// for anything that writes the card): an EDID's reading that is under way
// is let end first (the tick goes on meanwhile, `watching` set), then
// `watching` is cleared. At boot the kernel runs with interrupts off: no
// tick comes until the first program runs, and none is under way (the
// wait is for the steps asked for later, from the running system, where
// the tick is the timer's interrupt and runs to its end before the step
// goes on). False: a reading did not end in 2 s.
fn engineQuiet() -> bool {
    engineWatching = watching
    if !watching { return true }
    const deadline = clock.Deadline.after(2000)
    while true {
        watching = false
        if watch.reading == plug.NONE { return true }
        watching = true
        if deadline.passed() { return false }
        clock.pause(10000)
    }
    return false
}

// The card's engines brought as far as this boot says: its line first,
// then the stages (gpu_engines.mlx's `run`: S0, then M1 and M2, then I1;
// gpu_smc_sdma.mlx's: F1 and F2, then D1 and D2; each only after the one
// before it passed).
fn bringEngines(address: usize) -> void {
    engineSaid = true
    engineDevice = address
    const d = engineDecision()
    stages.say(displayWrite, &d, true)
    const hands = engines.Hands.{ .read = engineRead, .write = engineWrite, .now = displayNow, .pause = enginePause, .say = displayWrite, .aside = displayAside, .vramRead = engineVramRead, .vramWrite = engineVramWrite, .dma = dma, .memoryRead = engineMemoryRead, .command = engineCommand, .route = engineRoute, .interrupts = engineInterrupts, .waiting = engineWaiting, .file = engineFile }
    var where = engines.Card.{ .device = gpu.device, .revision = gpu.revision, .view = gpu.vramViewSize, .pictureOffset = gpu.consoleOffset, .pictureBytes = gpu.consoleBytes, .dmaBase = gpu.dmaBase, .dmaPages = if gpu.dmaBase != 0 { DMA_PAGES } else { 0 }, .rom = null }
    if tablesOpen { where.rom = &machine }
    engines.begin(&engineState, &hands, &where, d.stage)
    const more = loaded.More.{ .memoryWrite = engineMemoryWrite, .doorbell = engineDoorbell, .doorbells = if gpu.doorbellView != 0 { layout.Memory.HUGE / 4 } else { 0 } }
    loaded.begin(&loadedState, &more)
    if !engineQuiet() {
        console.out.write("gpu: the card's engines are not read at this boot: an EDID's reading did not end in 2 s\n")
        return
    }
    engines.run(&engineState)
    loaded.run(&engineState, &loadedState)
    engineBegun = true
    watching = engineWatching
}

// No card of ours for the stages: said in one line; an AMD card that was
// looked at but is not one whose stages run (not started, not a Polaris
// 10) is said so by S0 itself.
fn enginesWithoutCard() -> void {
    if gpu.registers != 0 && !gpu.present {
        bringEngines(0)
        return
    }
    engineSaid = true
    const d = engineDecision()
    stages.say(displayWrite, &d, false)
}

// The way down (power.mlx, before the machine restarts or powers off):
// what reads the system's memory stopped (the copy engines, the interrupt
// ring), the doorbells off, the GART left on. Nothing when no stage
// brought anything up.
pub fn stopEngines() -> void {
    if !engineBegun { return }
    watching = false
    loaded.stop(&engineState, &loadedState)
}

// ---- A mode set while the system runs (modes.mlx asks for it through
// the seam of display_modes.mlx; gpu_change.mlx is the change itself,
// with gpu_mode.mlx's plan and gpu_set.mlx's carrying out). New
// functions in a place of their own: nothing above is changed for them.
//
// The card's TMDS output (HDMI or DVI signalling) is given the mode
// asked for by the mode set that runs at boot, made for that mode's
// timing from what the card runs at that moment, with every other
// processor held still meanwhile (smp.mlx's `holdOthers`). A request
// writes the display's registers and runs the card's tables whatever
// this boot's choice was: the choice says what the boot does by itself,
// and a mode a program asks for later is asked for by a person, who gets
// the mode before back when the new one is not confirmed (modes.mlx).
// `native` is true for the time of the change and what it was
// afterwards. Any other output of the card (DisplayPort) has its modes
// listed and every request refused, with the reason.
const displays = @import("./displays.mlx")
const dm = @import("./display_modes.mlx")
const changing = @import("./gpu_change.mlx")
const together = @import("./gpu_together.mlx")

// What the driver knows of the card's display between changes, the
// tables as they are lent for one, and whether the first read-out for
// this part was made.
var seen: changing.Seen = undefined // (per run)
var seenTables: modeset.Tables = undefined // (per run)
var seenBegun = false // (per run)

// This boot's choice, for what goes by it elsewhere (a mode remembered
// from an earlier boot: modes.mlx).
pub fn bootChoice() -> *const choice.Decision { return &chosen }

// The kernel's display whose picture is in this card's memory (its
// number), displays.MAX when the card shows none of the kernel's or
// there is no card.
pub fn modeDisplay() -> usize {
    if !gpu.present { return displays.MAX }
    const screen = ownDisplay()
    return if screen.*.present { screen.*.number } else { displays.MAX }
}

// The monitor's EDID as this driver has read it itself over DDC from the
// output that shows the picture, every block of it (gpu_together.mlx's
// `modeEdid`: the last one there that holds); nothing while the driver
// reads none (modes.mlx then lists the modes from what the loader passed
// on).
pub fn modeEdid() -> []const u8 {
    if joined { return together.modeEdid(&driver) }
    const bytes = unsafe { @ptrFromInt([*]const u8, layout.Blob.virtual(layout.Blob.EDID)) }
    return bytes[0..0]
}

// For the log alone from here on (true), or as it was: what a change
// says step by step is kept off the serial line and the screen, where a
// program's own lines stand at that moment; the lines that say what was
// asked for and what came of it go there.
var changeAsideWas = false // (per run)

fn changeAside(on: bool) -> void {
    if on {
        changeAsideWas = console.logOnly(true)
    } else {
        _ = console.logOnly(changeAsideWas)
    }
}

fn changeHold() -> usize { return smp.holdOthers() }

fn changeRelease() -> void { smp.releaseOthers() }

// The card as a change is lent it.
fn changeLend() -> changing.Lent {
    var facts = mode.Rom.{ .machine = null, .board = null, .firmware = null }
    var tables: ?*const modeset.Tables = null
    if tablesOpen {
        facts.machine = &machine
        facts.board = &board
        facts.firmware = &firmware
        seenTables = modeset.Tables.{ .machine = &machine, .hands = tableHands(true), .aside = changeAside }
        tables = &seenTables
    }
    return changing.Lent.{ .card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }, .hands = modeset.Hands.{ .write = displayWriteRegister, .clear = displayClear }, .tables = tables, .rom = facts, .hold = changeHold, .release = changeRelease, .aside = changeAside }
}

// What the card runs now read for this part, once: after the boot's own
// mode set the read-out made before it is not the card's state any more.
fn changeBegin() -> void {
    if seenBegun { return }
    seenBegun = true
    changing.begin(&seen)
    const screen = ownDisplay()
    // (The display's part of the aperture: at a boot whose card's engines
    // went beyond stage 0 the driver has its top 64 MiB.)
    seen.memory.aperture = displayAperture()
    if gpu.consoleBytes != 0 && screen.*.present {
        seen.memory.pictureOffset = gpu.consoleOffset
        seen.memory.pictureBytes = gpu.consoleBytes
        seen.memory.pictureWidth = screen.*.width
        seen.memory.pictureHeight = screen.*.height
        seen.memory.picturePitch = screen.*.pitch / 4
    }
    seen.offer = offered
    if !gpu.polaris() { return }
    const lent = changeLend()
    const was = console.screen(false)
    changing.look(&lent, &seen)
    _ = console.screen(was)
}

// The rate the card's display runs at, as counted (frames in a thousand
// seconds; 0: not known).
pub fn modeRate() -> usize {
    if !gpu.present { return 0 }
    changeBegin()
    return changing.rate(&seen)
}

fn modeJudge(which: usize, asked: *dm.Mode) -> void {
    // (A new picture has to be cleared before it is shown: without the
    // card's memory in the kernel's view no mode is set.)
    if gpu.vramView == 0 || !joined {
        asked.*.why = dm.Why.CARD
        return
    }
    together.judge(&driver, asked)
}

// The change made, with the card written whatever this boot's choice
// was; when it ends done, the kernel's part of the card's memory and the
// display's picture are the new one's.
fn modeCarry(result: dm.Set, picture: *dm.Picture) -> dm.Set {
    if result != dm.Set.done { return result }
    gpu.consoleOffset = seen.plan.offset
    gpu.consoleBytes = seen.plan.pitch * 4 * seen.plan.height
    picture.*.address = gpu.vram + seen.plan.offset
    picture.*.width = seen.plan.width
    picture.*.height = seen.plan.height
    picture.*.pitch = seen.plan.pitch * 4
    return result
}

// (The one driver, gpu_together.mlx, has the card for the change: the
// hot plug tick kept off it, a reading under way let end, the switch's
// record brought up to date afterwards.)
fn modeSetTo(which: usize, asked: *const dm.Mode, picture: *dm.Picture) -> dm.Set {
    if !asked.*.timed || gpu.vramView == 0 || !joined { return dm.Set.refused }
    const was = native
    native = true
    const result = together.set(&driver, &asked.*.timing)
    native = was
    return modeCarry(result, picture)
}

fn modeBack(which: usize, before: *const dm.Mode, picture: *dm.Picture) -> dm.Set {
    if gpu.vramView == 0 || !joined { return dm.Set.refused }
    const was = native
    native = true
    const result = together.back(&driver, before)
    native = was
    return modeCarry(result, picture)
}

// An output whose mode this driver does not change, and a card whose
// display controller it does not know: every mode is judged so, and
// nothing is ever written for one.
fn modeNotOurs(which: usize, asked: *dm.Mode) -> void { asked.*.why = dm.Why.OUTPUT }

fn modeNoDriver(which: usize, asked: *dm.Mode) -> void { asked.*.why = dm.Why.NO_DRIVER }

fn modeNotSet(which: usize, asked: *const dm.Mode, picture: *dm.Picture) -> dm.Set { return dm.Set.refused }

fn modeNotBack(which: usize, before: *const dm.Mode, picture: *dm.Picture) -> dm.Set { return dm.Set.refused }

// The seam's record for the card's display: its TMDS output's driver
// when the picture leaves by one (a card whose ROM is not open, or whose
// memory is not in the kernel's view, has every mode judged as one the
// card's state does not allow); for another output one that lists and
// refuses; for a card whose display controller is not known here (no
// Polaris) the same, as for a display no driver sets.
pub fn modeDriver() -> dm.Driver {
    changeBegin()
    if !gpu.polaris() {
        return dm.Driver.{ .kind = dm.Kind.FIRMWARE, .which = 0, .timed = false, .anySize = false, .judge = modeNoDriver, .set = modeNotSet, .back = modeNotBack }
    }
    if changing.tmds(&seen) {
        return dm.Driver.{ .kind = dm.Kind.RADEON_TMDS, .which = 0, .timed = true, .anySize = false, .judge = modeJudge, .set = modeSetTo, .back = modeBack }
    }
    return dm.Driver.{ .kind = dm.Kind.RADEON_OTHER, .which = 0, .timed = true, .anySize = false, .judge = modeNotOurs, .set = modeNotSet, .back = modeNotBack }
}

// How long the other processors stood still for the last change, in
// nanoseconds (0: none was made yet).
pub fn modeStood() -> usize { return if seenBegun { seen.stood } else { 0 } }

// ---- The mode change, the hot plug tick and the switch as one driver
// (gpu_together.mlx says how they keep off one another and what each
// leaves in the others' records). New functions in a place of their own.

// The one driver, begun (`joined`) at the end of `take` for a Polaris card
// whose display this driver reads; what it lends the switch's move (the
// mode change's machinery and its record: one record of the card's
// display) and the DDC's hands.
var driver: together.Driver = undefined // (per run)
var joined = false // (per run)
var joinedLent: changing.Lent = undefined // (per run)
var switchLent: rule.Lent = undefined // (per run)
var switchHands: modeset.Hands = undefined // (per run)
var switchFacts: mode.Rom = undefined // (per run)
var joinedDdc: ddc.Hands = undefined // (per run)

// The driver begun once the boot's own mode set, the record of the
// monitor followed and the engines' stages are over: the mode change's
// record read from the card as it runs now (`changeBegin`), the switch's
// move lent that record and the same machinery.
fn joinUp() -> void {
    changeBegin()
    joinedLent = changeLend()
    switchHands = joinedLent.hands
    switchFacts = joinedLent.rom
    switchLent = rule.Lent.{ .hands = &switchHands, .tables = joinedLent.tables, .rom = &switchFacts, .memory = &seen.memory, .readout = &seen.readout, .plan = &seen.plan, .result = &seen.result, .change = &joinedLent, .seen = &seen }
    joinedDdc = ddc.Hands.{ .write = ddcWriteRegister }
    driver.card = scanout.Card.{ .read = displayRead, .now = displayNow, .write = displayWrite }
    driver.watch = &watch
    driver.follow = &following
    driver.lent = &switchLent
    driver.change = &joinedLent
    driver.seen = &seen
    driver.hands = null
    if asking { driver.hands = &joinedDdc }
    driver.pause = ddcPause
    driver.watching = &watching
    together.begin(&driver)
    joined = true
}

// The change the timer's tick found due (the switch's move, or its mode
// set again), made once the interrupt is over: modes.mlx's `due` calls
// this on the trap's way back. `state`: the display's state of the modes,
// whose pending mode is taken back first. What came of it.
pub fn carryDue(state: ?*dm.State) -> together.Due {
    if !joined { return together.Due.none }
    return together.carryDue(&driver, state)
}

// Whether such a change is due.
pub fn changeDue() -> bool { return joined && together.isDue(&driver) }

// How often the monitor whose EDID the display's modes are listed from
// became another (modes.mlx lists them anew when this moved on).
pub fn monitorChanges() -> usize { return if joined { driver.monitors } else { 0 } }

// ---- For programs (gpu_programs.mlx: the card as /devices/gpu0): what
// the engines' first look read at this boot (null: it read nothing of
// this card).
pub fn enginesRead() -> ?*const engines.Engines {
    if !engineBegun { return null }
    return &engineState
}
